  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 204 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nSouth Florida will remain situated between split upper-level high \npressure over the western Atlantic and the Gulf, before ridging re-\nestablishes across the eastern CONUS early next week. At the \nsurface, high pressure over the western Atlantic will maintain a \nlight easterly flow regime across the peninsula through the forecast \nperiod. Overall, a typical wet season pattern is expected to \ncontinue, with early morning showers and isolated storms developing \nalong the East Coast before activity gradually shifts inland, \npeaking across inland and SW Florida each afternoon. \n\nBouts of drier air will attempt to filter into the region through \nthe weekend as a Saharan dust plume approaches the region, with the \nhighest concentrations expected to arrive early next week. While the \ncore of the dust plume is forecast to remain south of the peninsula, \nmid-level dry air intrusion at its periphery could steepen lapse \nrates and increase DCAPE locally, enhancing the threat for severe \nwind gusts within robust afternoon convection. Conversely, greater \nconcentrations of Saharan dust overspreading the region could act to \nsuppress overall convective activity to some degree. Guidance \ncontinues to suggest that PWATs will remain close to slightly \nabove climatology through the forecast period, indicating that \nsufficient moisture will be available to support at least \nscattered daily convection through the long term. Outside of any \ninfluence from the Saharan dust plume, the potential for severe \nweather is expected to remain fairly limited, with wind shear \nstaying weak and 500 mb temperatures forecast to remain near to \nslightly warmer than climatology. Given the uncertainty regarding \nthe exact timing and extent of the dry air intrusion, trends will \ncontinue to be monitored. \n\nHigh temperatures will rise into the lower 90s across most areas \nthrough early next week, reaching the mid 90s over interior SW \nFlorida. Overnight lows will range from the low 70s across the \ninterior to the low 80s along the coasts. Major HeatRisk \nprobabilities increase into the weekend as onshore flow maintains \nelevated heat indices and limits overnight cooling, then decrease \nearly next week as drier air arrives.",
